sl7-sensors (1.1.0) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * New per-user sl7-backlight-resync.service: GNOME 50 moved brightness
    into mutter, whose cached Backlight value never tracks external sysfs
    writes — with the direct controller the slider lies and brightness
    keys step from a stale value (up can visibly dim). The watcher polls
    sysfs and refreshes mutter's cache with a value-preserving
    SetBacklight whenever they diverge — including mid-ramp, so the
    slider tracks auto-brightness live (the controller recognizes the
    echo of its own recent writes). Watches gsd's SetDimming and relays
    dim windows to the controller: dim writes are policy, not
    calibration, and GNOME 50 never un-dims the sysfs backlight itself —
    so during a dim the stack pauses, and on dim-end the controller
    restores by ramping back to the light-appropriate level. Idles on
    non-GNOME sessions; self-obsoletes once mutter is fixed.
  * Controller: the single calibration anchor becomes a multi-point
    lux->brightness curve. Slider/key corrections warp the curve locally
    in log-light (zero effect beyond ~50x the corrected lux) and insert
    an exact point — a night fix no longer re-tunes daylight. The old
    anchor migrates automatically with identical behavior.
  * Controller: deferred anchoring — a correction freezes brightness at
    the chosen value immediately but is paired with the light level only
    once the reading settles. Fixes corrections decaying when made just
    after moving somewhere darker or brighter.
  * New 'sl7-sensors curve' shows the learned calibration; status reports
    the resync watcher and the curve point count.
  * Controller mode hides the ALS on D-Bus (HasAmbientLight=false;
    LightLevel stays published for passive readers). That property is what
    makes gsd-power claim the sensor and drive the panel itself — two
    controllers on one backlight, and every gsd write reached ours as a
    fake user correction. Now nothing else can engage, whatever any
    desktop's ambient-enabled says.
  * Controller: auto-brightness on the login screen, without learning
    there. The GDM greeter ran its own gsd-power auto-brightness against
    this proxy — it claims light and then writes the backlight itself,
    and every one of those writes was adopted as a user correction,
    engraving a bogus curve point on every single boot (and evicting a
    real one). The controller now tracks logind's active session: while
    its class is 'greeter' it still ramps (nothing else drives the panel
    now, so the login screen finally follows the room), but it adopts
    nothing — a calibration point means a user chose it, and there is no
    user yet. It reclaims the panel when a user session becomes active.
  * The QMI/QRTR framing and its protobuf codec move to a shared ssc.py in
    the private libdir: sensor-proxy and read-sensor each carried a copy
    (~110 identical lines) kept in step by a test. The module stays
    gi-free so 'sl7-sensors lux' still works without python3-gi.
  * Controller mode no longer emits LightLevel PropertiesChanged when no
    client has claimed: with the ALS hidden nothing subscribes, so that
    was ~115k D-Bus signals a day to nobody. Properties.Get is unchanged.
  * The ramp timer now runs only while there is something to ramp. At
    10 Hz it was two thirds of the daemon's continuous CPU, spent
    re-reading an unchanged sysfs file for the many hours a day the panel
    already sits at target; it is re-armed by a new target or by the 1 s
    poll noticing a gap. The echo ring ages in wall time (CTRL_ECHO_S)
    rather than tick counts, which a stopped timer would have frozen.
  * The lux EMA measures the interval between arrivals instead of assuming
    the requested rate. Delivery is a fixed ~1.33 Hz however fast the DSP
    is asked, so every time constant was about 3x longer than it claimed
    (TAU_SLOW behaved like ~18 s), and a gap after suspend crawled out of
    a stale value instead of re-seeding.
  * backlight-resync: skip the mutter round-trip when sysfs still holds
    the value mutter last took from us — that query ran every second all
    day, waking gnome-shell each time, for a value that moves rarely. An
    unconditional check every RESYNC_FULL_EVERY polls backstops a cache
    we did not predict.
  * sensor-proxy: use register_object_with_closures2 where GLib has it,
    silencing the deprecation warning logged on every service start.
  * Controller: claim the panel's pre-existing brightness at first light,
    so the level a daemon restart finds is never mistaken for a
    correction.

 -- valeronm <valeriy@hey.com>  Tue, 21 Jul 2026 22:30:00 +0100

sl7-sensors (1.0.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * sensor-proxy: close the SSC sensor stream across suspend. The ADSP
    keeps pushing indications while the host sleeps and every one is an
    IPCC wakeup — with the stream open, suspend dies within one push
    interval (~250 ms at 4 Hz). Hold a logind delay inhibitor, tear the
    stream down on PrepareForSleep (bypassing the controller guard),
    resubscribe on resume (controller or active claims only).

 -- valeronm <valeriy@hey.com>  Tue, 21 Jul 2026 18:57:34 +0100
sl7-sensors (1.0.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * status: oneshot-aware unit reporting — FAILED with a journalctl
    pointer, "not installed" (LoadState), and "skipped — config not
    extracted (run 'sl7-sensors setup')" when the start condition gated
    the unit, instead of raw systemctl is-active output.
  * Usage goes to stderr on error exits; mode errors carry the
    "sl7-sensors mode: error:" prefix; usage documents setup's
    [windows-root] argument and points at the man page.
  * color stream: clamp channels at 0 — rounding a tiny negative float
    printed "rgb=(3,2,-0)".

 -- valeronm <valeriy@hey.com>  Mon, 20 Jul 2026 23:45:00 +0100

sl7-sensors (1.0.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Test suite: 72 pytest cases — QMI/protobuf framing (with a drift guard
    between read-sensor and sensor-proxy), EMA filter, backlight
    controller math and corrupt-anchor handling, set-mode rewrite, CLI,
    and the setup extraction end-to-end against a fake DriverStore.
    Wired into the package build via dh_auto_test.
  * Systemd sandbox for sl7-sensors.service (ReadWritePaths for the
    DSP-written registry; no PrivateDevices — needs /dev/fastrpc-adsp).
  * Lintian-clean at --fail-on error,warning: sl7-sensors(1) man page,
    libtool build-tree RUNPATH stripped (chrpath), bindnow hardening,
    orphaned GPL-3+ stanza dropped from debian/copyright.
  * Removed the pre-release ssc-sensor-proxy migration from postinst.
  * CI: two-job least-privilege workflow shared with sl7-mac — static
    checks, tag/changelog gate, lintian gate, create-or-update release.
  * Polish: absolute python3 shebangs, prefixed error messages, idle-dim
    caveat and Releases pointer in README.

 -- valeronm <valeriy@hey.com>  Mon, 20 Jul 2026 22:55:00 +0100

sl7-sensors (1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release: SSC sensor bring-up for the Surface Laptop 7 —
    sensors-PD FastRPC file service feeding the DSP registry the sensor
    configuration extracted from the machine's Windows partition,
    net.hadess.SensorProxy daemon with optional direct backlight
    controller, and the unified sl7-sensors CLI (setup, lux, color, mode,
    status).

